Three things are not covered by it, and one of them matters when you ship.


FFmpeg is a submodule, not part of this repository
--------------------------------------------------

`FFmpeg/` is a git submodule. Clone without `--recursive` and it is not there at all. Its contents
are licensed by the FFmpeg project, not by this one, and how they are licensed depends on how you
configure the build.

The configuration used here — see the *Make FFmpeg* build phase — passes `--enable-gpl` and
`--enable-version3`, which makes the resulting FFmpeg libraries **GPL v3**. Without those flags
FFmpeg builds under the LGPL.


The codec libraries are fetched at build time
---------------------------------------------

The build copies static archives out of Homebrew — see `CODEC_LIBRARIES` in the *Make FFmpeg* build
phase, which is the list that decides this. None of them is redistributed in this repository, and
each is present only because the matching `--enable-` flag is passed.

| Library            | Homebrew formula | Licence                       |
|--------------------|------------------|-------------------------------|
| x264               | `x264`           | GPL v2 or later               |
| x265               | `x265`           | GPL v2 or later               |
| LAME (mp3)         | `lame`           | LGPL v2.1 or later            |
| libvpx (VP8/VP9)   | `libvpx`         | BSD 3-clause                  |
| libopus            | `opus`           | BSD 3-clause                  |
| libogg             | `libogg`         | BSD 3-clause                  |
| libvorbis          | `libvorbis`      | BSD 3-clause                  |
| libaom (AV1)       | `aom`            | BSD 2-clause + patent grant   |
| libvmaf            | `libvmaf`        | BSD 2-clause + patent grant   |

Only x264 and x265 are copyleft. Everything else on that list is permissive and adds no obligation
of its own, so the outcome below is set by x264, x265 and FFmpeg's own `--enable-gpl`, exactly as it
was before those libraries were added.

`zlib` and `bz2` also come in, from the macOS SDK rather than Homebrew, under the zlib and BSD
licences respectively.

Nothing non-free is on that list, and nothing non-free belongs on it. `libfdk-aac` in particular
produces better AAC than the native encoder but is distributed under terms the GPL cannot absorb,
which would make the binary undistributable rather than merely copyleft.


A built FFmpegTask binary is GPL v3
-----------------------------------

This is the one that has consequences. FFmpegTask statically links FFmpeg and every library in the
table above, so the **binary** is a combined work and must be distributed under the GPL — regardless of the MIT licence
on the sources that went into it. Shipping `XPCFFmpegService.xpc` inside an application means
distributing that binary, with the obligations that carries: preserve the notices, ship the licence
text, and make the corresponding source available. This repository, being public, is that source.

Nothing else built from this repository contains FFmpeg code. The application, the XPC service and
`XPCFFmpeg.framework` have zero FFmpeg symbols, which you can check for yourself:

    nm -U YourApp.app/Contents/MacOS/YourApp | grep -cE '_av_|_avcodec_|_x264_|_x265_'   # 0

That separation is deliberate, and it is why an application using this service does not have to
publish its own source. The reasoning is in FFmpegTask/README.md.

If you would rather not ship GPL code at all, build FFmpeg without `--enable-gpl`,
`--enable-version3`, `--enable-libx264` and `--enable-libx265`, and use the VideoToolbox encoders.
The permissive libraries in the table can all stay: LAME, libvpx, libopus, libvorbis and libaom are
LGPL or BSD, so an LGPL FFmpeg build keeps mp3, VP8/VP9, Opus, Vorbis and AV1 encoding. The MIT
sources here are unchanged by that choice; only what they link against changes.
